        <article class="min-w-0 flex-1 space-y-16 pb-24 text-[15px] leading-7 text-gray-700">

            <header>
                <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">Documentation</p>
                <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900">How Windcraft works</h1>
                <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-gray-600">The gallery, projects, the editor and agents, step by step. If a word looks unfamiliar, check the <a href="#glossary" class="font-medium text-gray-900 underline">glossary</a> at the bottom.</p>
            </header>

            <section id="what" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">What is Windcraft?</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">Windcraft has two doors. The <strong class="text-gray-900">gallery</strong> is where you come to look at designs and choose one. The <strong class="text-gray-900">editor</strong> is where you make a design, with its code beside it. Every design lives in a <strong class="text-gray-900">project</strong>.</p>
                <p class="mt-3">It is for developers who want code to copy, designers who want to work visually, teams who want one look across a product, and anyone building with an AI coding tool. It is free.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="start" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Quick start</h2>
                <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li>Open the <a href="/" class="font-medium underline">gallery</a>, pick a category, and look at a design. Press <em>Code</em> to see it, and the copy button to take it. No account needed.</li><li>Press <strong class="text-gray-900">Open in editor</strong> to make your own copy, or <strong class="text-gray-900">Save</strong> to keep it. Both ask which project it goes in.</li><li>Or press <strong class="text-gray-900">New design</strong> in the top bar: a dialog opens with the code on the left and the design on the right. Write or paste code, press Save, and choose its project. (On the Projects page the top bar has <strong class="text-gray-900">New project</strong>, which makes a project and opens the editor on a blank page with the code panel below it.)</li><li>Set the project's look (colours, font, corners) on its page, then turn <strong class="text-gray-900">Public</strong> on for any design you want to share in the gallery.</li></ol>
            </section>

            <section id="gallery" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">The gallery</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">The gallery is organised by category.</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Preview and Code</strong>: switch between how a design looks and its code. Pick Desktop, Tablet or Mobile in the pill beside Preview and Code (the last two in a device frame, at the device's real screen size), light or dark, or full screen.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Copy</strong>: copy the code. You need no account for this.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Save</strong> and <strong class="text-gray-900">Open in editor</strong>, under the three dots at the end of the toolbar: Save puts a private copy into one of your projects; Open in editor makes your own copy in a project and opens it.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Pages and Components</strong>: once the gallery holds both, tabs at the top show all designs, only whole pages, or only components.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section id="projects" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Projects</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">A project is where everything starts: a design cannot exist outside one. It is a folder for your pages and components, with one shared look, a share link, and a place to export from.</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">New project</strong> (top bar) creates it at once and opens the editor on a blank page. Rename it on its page.</li><li>The <strong class="text-gray-900">Projects</strong> page lists your projects as cards, each with a preview of its frames, its tags and how many frames it has. Search by name or tag in the top bar, tick one or more categories in the <em>All Categories</em> dropdown, and press tag chips below to narrow further. A project shows when it matches any ticked category and any chosen tag.</li><li>The project page shows a board of every frame: a page or a component, each one something you can preview. Drag frames to arrange them. The <strong class="text-gray-900">Frames</strong> tab lists them, and the <em>This is a page</em> switch in a frame's details says whether it is a page or a component.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Elements of a frame</strong> are found for you: the header, hero, sections, footer, forms and buttons of each frame, listed in the <strong class="text-gray-900">Elements</strong> tab as "from <em>frame name</em>". They update when the frame is saved. The <em>Auto-create elements</em> switch in the project's settings turns this on or off.</li><li>Select an item to open its details: set its <strong class="text-gray-900">category</strong> and <strong class="text-gray-900">tags</strong>, turn <strong class="text-gray-900">Public</strong> on or off, <strong class="text-gray-900">export</strong> it, see its <strong class="text-gray-900">history</strong> and restore a version, <strong class="text-gray-900">move</strong> it to another project, tick <strong class="text-gray-900">Use as AI reference</strong>, or <strong class="text-gray-900">delete</strong> it. Search, a category filter and tag chips narrow the list.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Import</strong> adds designs from HTML files, a zip, or pasted HTML. Long pages can be split into sections.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Design guide</strong> (Projects page) downloads a markdown summary of your designs, for a CLAUDE.md or an agent.</li><li>Deleting a project deletes everything in it.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section id="editor" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">The editor</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">The editor shows the design and its code side by side. Edit either: change the code and the design follows, drag or restyle on the canvas and the code follows.</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Tools</strong>: select, hand, interact (try the page as it will run), frame, shapes, text, and an add menu.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Preview</strong> (the play button) opens the project's <strong class="text-gray-900">prototype</strong> in its own tab, at <code>/prototype/…</code>: every page, one at a time and full screen, the way a visitor sees it. It shows the saved version of your pages. The page fills the screen, with a toolbar floating over it at the top right (drag it by its handle, or minimize it to a small chip) that has the arrows (or the Left and Right keys) to move between pages, <strong class="text-gray-900">Share</strong>, and a scaling menu (Actual size, Responsive (Z), Desktop, Tablet and Mobile (the last two in a phone or tablet frame, at the device's real screen size), Fit width, Fit width and height, Fill screen, and Hide UI with Cmd or Ctrl and \\). Share makes a link anyone can open to click through the pages (no code, no editing, no account). <em>Stop sharing</em> in the same box ends the link. The Interact tool is different: it runs the page you are editing, right on the canvas.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Code panel</strong>: syntax colours, auto-closing tags, find and replace, Tailwind class suggestions, and <strong class="text-gray-900">Format</strong> to tidy it. It sits below the design by default; the dock button moves it to the left, and your choice is remembered.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Properties</strong>: edit an element's spacing, size, type, colour, border and effects. Each value is written to where it comes from (a Tailwind class or CSS).</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Save</strong> opens a small form under the toolbar: name, category (which decides page or component), project, description, tags, and visibility. A new project's first save asks only for a name.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">New design</strong> in the gallery's top bar opens the same editor in a dialog, with a button to open it in the full editor.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Screen sizes</strong>: pick Mobile, Tablet or Desktop on the canvas. Edits are written for the size you are on: Mobile as plain classes, Tablet with <code>md:</code>, Desktop with <code>lg:</code>.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Interactions</strong>: select a button and say what it does: show or hide something, change classes, scroll, copy, or go to another frame. Tabs and Modal are one click. An arrow on the canvas shows what a button acts on.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Animations</strong>: a preset library, a keyframe editor, scroll-linked effects, text effects and SVG drawing. The keyframes go into your Tailwind config.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Config tab</strong>: edit <code>tailwind.config.js</code> (Tailwind 3) or <code>global.css</code> (Tailwind 4), with plugins. It applies as you type.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section id="theme" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">A project's look</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">On the project page, the <strong class="text-gray-900">Theme</strong> tab holds four choices that the editor, every preview and your exports use:</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Brand colour</strong>: one colour. Its eleven shades become <code>brand-50</code> to <code>brand-950</code>, and the colour itself is <code>brand-600</code>.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Gray</strong>: which of Tailwind's five grays <code>gray-*</code> means here.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Font</strong>: a Google Fonts family, or the system font.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Corners</strong>: how round <code>rounded-*</code> is.</li></ul>
                <p class="mt-3">Change it once and every page and component in the project follows.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="share" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Publishing and sharing</h2>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Public</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">Designs are private. Turn <strong class="text-gray-900">Public</strong> on for a design on its project page and it appears in the gallery, under its category, where anyone can look at it and copy the code.</p>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Share link</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">The <strong class="text-gray-900">Share</strong> button on a project page makes a link. Anyone who opens it can look at the whole project in its theme and copy the code, with no account. Nothing in it can be changed. <strong class="text-gray-900">Stop sharing</strong> ends the link.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="export" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Export and history</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">Any design can be copied or exported as HTML, React, Vue or Svelte, and as a design spec or a profile. The page code stays plain HTML with Tailwind, so you are never locked in.</p>
                <p class="mt-3">To keep the project's look in exported code, take the theme setup too: it is a small Tailwind config for the brand colour, gray, font and corners.</p>
                <p class="mt-3">Every change keeps the earlier version. Open an item's <em>History</em> to see them and restore one.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="agents" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Connect your tools</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">The <a href="/connect" class="font-medium underline">Connect</a> page (in the sidebar, signed in) lets AI tools read and write your projects, instead of guessing. At the top is the <strong class="text-gray-900">MCP endpoint</strong>: the one address every tool uses, with a Copy button and a <strong class="text-gray-900">Test connection</strong> button that checks it. Pick your tool in the tabs below it and follow its short guide. All of it is optional.</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Claude (web and Desktop)</strong>: add Windcraft as a custom connector in claude.ai, paste the endpoint, and allow access. No token is needed. This needs a public HTTPS address, so it does not work against localhost.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Claude Code</strong>: put your access token in an environment variable, then run the <code>claude mcp add</code> command shown on the page. A downloadable kit adds a project config and a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> section.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Cursor</strong>: press <em>Add to Cursor</em> on the page, or add the <code>mcp.json</code> shown there. It reads your token from an environment variable.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">ChatGPT</strong>: add Windcraft as a custom connector (developer mode), paste the endpoint and sign in. No token is needed, and it needs a public HTTPS address.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Other clients</strong> (VS Code, Windsurf and more): use the endpoint over HTTP with an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header and your token.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900"><a href="https://www.figma.com/community/plugin/1697363010304569719" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline">Figma plugin</a></strong>: sign in with your account once, then send a design into a project as editable code.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Design pack</strong>: download your design guide and the designs marked <em>Use as AI reference</em>, and attach them in Claude Design.</li></ul>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Access token</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">Claude Code, Cursor and other MCP clients sign in with an access token. Create it in the box beside the tabs on the Connect page, or in <a href="/settings" class="font-medium underline">Settings</a>. It is shown once when you create it; you can regenerate it or revoke it, and either stops the old one at once. <strong class="text-gray-900">Treat it like a password</strong>: anyone who has it can read and add to your projects. Keep it in an environment variable or your tool's secret settings, never in a file you share. The <em>Connected</em> list on the page shows the apps and Figma sign-ins with access, each with a Disconnect button.</p>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Prompts and resources</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">Besides tools, Windcraft offers <strong class="text-gray-900">prompts</strong>, ready-made commands your client shows when you type <code>/</code>: <em>build-page</em>, <em>restyle-to-my-guide</em>, <em>audit-design</em>, <em>make-variations</em> and <em>new-section</em>. It also offers <strong class="text-gray-900">resources</strong> you can attach to a chat: your design guide, the active project, a project's pages and its Tailwind theme.</p>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">Seeing and undoing what an AI did</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">A design's <strong class="text-gray-900">History</strong> marks the versions an AI made with an <em>Edited by</em> badge. The Connect page lists <strong class="text-gray-900">Recent AI activity</strong>, the last ten things connected tools did, each with a link and an <strong class="text-gray-900">Undo</strong> button. Undo puts the design back to how it was before that change, and is offered only while you haven't edited the design since, so it never removes your own work.</p>
                <h3 class="mt-6 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">What the agent can do</h3>
                <p class="mt-3">The <strong class="text-gray-900">Tools</strong> tab on the Connect page lists every tool the server offers, each marked read only or changes data. An agent can read your design guide, search and fetch your designs (as HTML, React, Vue or Svelte), edit a saved design with small precise changes, and save new components, pages and projects. Every save goes into a project. The full list:</p>
                <div class="mt-3 divide-y divide-gray-200 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white text-sm"><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">get_design_guide</code><span>A summary of your designs: colours, fonts, spacing and an index. Agents read it first.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">get_context</code><span>Which account and active project the agent is working in.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">list_projects, get_project, create_project, update_project, set_active_project</code><span>See, create and change projects (name, description, brief), and choose the one saves go to.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">search_components</code><span>Find designs by keyword or tag.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">get_component</code><span>Get one design as HTML, React, Vue or Svelte.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">get_design_spec</code><span>Look at a design as a labelled tree of elements.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">apply_design_patch</code><span>Make small changes to a design by element id.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">save_component, save_design, save_pages</code><span>Save a component, a finished page, or several pages. Every save goes into a project: name one, or set an active project.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">render_design</code><span>A screenshot of a design, so the agent can look at its own work and fix it. Needs a browser on the server.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">revert_design</code><span>Undo the latest AI change to a design (never your own edit), or go back to a version you name.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">search, fetch</code><span>Find a design and read it, in the shape ChatGPT expects.</span></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-3 sm:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]"><code class="font-mono text-gray-900">list_tags</code><span>List your tags.</span></div></div>
            </section>

            <section id="npm" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Use the editor on your site</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">The editor is also a package, <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/windcraft-editor" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-medium underline">windcraft-editor</a> on npm, that you can put in any web page. It needs no backend and no build step. Choose how much you need:</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900"><code>startEditor</code></strong>: the editor as the whole page. Designs are kept in the browser.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900"><code>mountEditor</code></strong>: the same, saving to your own server through a few functions you write (the <em>host</em>).</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900"><code>embedEditor</code></strong>: the editor in one box of a page, such as a form field. Read and set its HTML from your code.</li></ul>
                <p class="mt-3">Try it in one file. Save this as <code>index.html</code>, run <code>npx serve</code> next to it and open the address:</p>
                <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl bg-gray-900 p-4 text-[13px] leading-6 text-gray-100"><code>&lt;div id="root"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;script type="module"&gt;
  import { startEditor } from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/windcraft-editor/editor/index.js";
  startEditor(document.getElementById("root"), {
    html: '&lt;h1 class="p-10 text-4xl font-bold"&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;',
  });
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
                <p class="mt-3">The full guides are on GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/Tolaj/windcraft/blob/main/docs/editor/getting-started.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-medium underline">getting started</a> (npm, React, Vue, Next, Vite), the <a href="https://github.com/Tolaj/windcraft/blob/main/docs/editor/api.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-medium underline">API reference</a>, the <a href="https://github.com/Tolaj/windcraft/blob/main/docs/editor/host.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-medium underline">host reference</a> with a server example, and <a href="https://github.com/Tolaj/windcraft/blob/main/docs/editor/recipes.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-medium underline">recipes</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="account" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Your account</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">Create an account with an email and password, or choose <strong class="text-gray-900">Continue with Google</strong> on the sign-in page. A Google sign-in uses the same email, so it joins an existing account with that address. Open <a href="/settings" class="font-medium underline">Settings</a> from your avatar to manage it:</p>
                <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Account</strong>: your name, your email and how you sign in.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Password</strong>: change it, or add one if you signed up with Google.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">AI generation</strong>: switch it on and choose your Anthropic key or a local model. It stays off until you do.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Access token</strong>: create, regenerate or revoke the token that Claude Code and other MCP clients use.</li><li><strong class="text-gray-900">Delete account</strong>: removes your account, projects, designs and tokens for good. You confirm with your email, and your password if you have one.</li></ul>
            </section>

            <section id="glossary" class="scroll-mt-24">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900">Glossary</h2>
                <dl class="mt-4 divide-y divide-gray-200 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white text-sm"><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Gallery</dt><dd>Public designs, by category, most liked first.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Project</dt><dd>Where designs live: a folder with a shared look, a share link and exports.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Page and component</dt><dd>Both are frames. A page is a whole screen; a component is a reusable piece you keep. Both live in a project.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Element</dt><dd>A piece found inside a frame, such as its header, hero or footer. Elements are not frames of their own.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Theme</dt><dd>A project's brand colour, gray, font and corners.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Public</dt><dd>A design turned on for the gallery. Private by default.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Share link</dt><dd>A view-only link to a whole project.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">MCP</dt><dd>The standard that lets coding tools use your projects.</dd></div><div class="grid gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[160px_1fr]"><dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">Access token</dt><dd>A secret that lets a tool like Claude Code use your projects. Claude on the web and the Figma plugin sign in instead.</dd></div></dl>
                <p class="mt-6 text-sm">Still stuck? See <a href="/support" class="font-medium underline">Support</a>.</p>
            </section>

        </article>
    </div>
</main>

<script>
    (function () {
        var links = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('#toc .toc-link'));
        var sections = links.map(function (a) { return document.getElementById(a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)); });

        function update() {
            var current = 0;
            var atBottom = window.innerHeight + window.scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 4;
            if (atBottom) {
                current = sections.length - 1;
            } else {
                sections.forEach(function (sec, i) {
                    if (sec && sec.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 140) current = i;
                });
            }
            links.forEach(function (a, i) { a.classList.toggle('is-active', i === current); });
        }

        window.addEventListener('scroll', update, { passive: true });
        window.addEventListener('resize', update);
        update();
    })();
</script>
